  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., one where a larger portion of the probability mass is on unfavorable outcomes, typically a few high-payoff outcomes with low probability and a high probability of low or zero payoff). To assess skewness, we need to compare the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 otherwise. Lottery B offers 6000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 otherwise. Both lotteries have a single positive outcome and zero otherwise, so both are highly right-skewed by nature. However, Lottery B has a higher payoff (6000 vs 3000) but half the probability (0.1% vs 0.2%), meaning it concentrates even more probability on the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) than A. Thus, B assigns 99.9% to 0, while A assigns 99.8% to 0. Therefore, Lottery B is more right-skewed than A (higher probability of nothing, and a more extreme low-probability high outcome). The decision reason would prefer B because it is more right-skewed.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports that they chose Lottery A because the probability of winning was higher in A (0.2%) compared to B (0.1%). They explicitly state that they wanted to stay on the safe side and keep risk low by selecting the option with a higher chance of winning. This indicates that the individual focused on the probability of a non-zero outcome and equated a higher chance of winning with lower risk. Their justification centers on maximizing the chance of a positive outcome and minimizing risk in terms of avoiding a zero outcome. They did not mention outcome magnitudes, skewness, or the distributional shape of the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness and prefers the more right-skewed lottery (B), which has a lower probability of winning but a higher payoff. The individual, however, explicitly based their decision on the higher probability of winning in A and sought to minimize risk by increasing the chance of a non-zero outcome. This is conceptually opposite to preferring greater right-skewness, which involves accepting even lower probabilities of winning for higher payoffs. The individual considered only the likelihood of winning, not the shape of the distribution in terms of skewness. There is no indication that they evaluated or preferred more extreme low-probability, high-payoff structures. Therefore, the information used by the individual (probability of any win) and their justification (risk minimization via higher win chance) are inconsistent with the skewness-based reason, which would favor more extreme skew (i.e., B).",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
